      "claim": "The page presents String.length() as a character count and charAt() as returning a character without stating that Java indexes UTF-16 char values (code units). For supplementary Unicode characters, length() can be 2 and charAt() returns one surrogate, so the unqualified objective and cheat sheet are technically false.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "url": "https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/java/lang/String.html",
          "section": "Class description; length(); charAt(int); codePointAt(int)",
          "retrievedAt": "2026-10-13T18:02:44Z",
          "textSha256": "d6fe54ea59fc2c32b57df32f03ec3af06d0c95043fb1c284d7d30a890b5729e2"
        }
      ],
      "recommendation": "Scope the current statements explicitly to the ASCII examples, and add one concise accuracy note that String indexes and length count UTF-16 char values; charAt returns one char value and code-point APIs are needed for general Unicode text.",
      "reviewedSha256": "5930cba8178219d767828ec4d4b489f0dccc1d6df3e6bb52beb97164076ae100"
